---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'State Spousal Support Guidelines | spousalsupportcalc.com';
const description = 'State-by-state spousal support frameworks: formula states, multi-factor states, and caps on duration and amount.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>State Spousal Support Guidelines</h1>
  <p>Spousal support law varies dramatically by state. Below are notable approaches.</p>

  <h2>Formula-based states for temporary or permanent support</h2>

  <h3>California</h3>
  <p>Temporary: typically uses the Santa Clara or county-specific formula (40% of higher net minus 50% of lower net, with adjustments). Permanent: judicial discretion under Family Code &sect; 4320 factors. Long marriages (10+ years) get indefinite support more readily.</p>

  <h3>Massachusetts</h3>
  <p>Statutory formula: 30&ndash;35% of the income gap. Duration tied to marriage length (e.g., 50% for 10-year marriage, 80% for 20-year marriage). Cap at recipient's "need" defined as living standard during marriage.</p>

  <h3>Pennsylvania</h3>
  <p>Formula similar to child support guidelines: 33% of higher net minus 40% of lower net (no children scenario), adjusted when children are involved.</p>

  <h3>Texas</h3>
  <p>Statutory cap: lesser of $5,000/month or 20% of supporting spouse's average monthly gross income. Duration cap: 5 years for most cases, longer for very long marriages or disability.</p>

  <h3>Illinois</h3>
  <p>Formula: 33.3% of payer's net income minus 25% of recipient's net income. Duration calculated as a percentage of marriage length (e.g., 24% for 5-year marriage, 60% for 15-year marriage).</p>

  <h3>New York</h3>
  <p>Temporary support uses a statutory formula (30% of higher income minus 20% of lower, with caps). Post-divorce (permanent) follows the Maintenance Guidelines Act with similar formulas but capped at a $203,000 income guideline.</p>

  <h2>Discretion-heavy states</h2>
  <p>Most other states use multi-factor analysis without a binding formula. Outcomes vary widely by judge and county.</p>
  <p>Notable examples: Florida (with caps on duration tied to marriage type), Georgia, Arizona (formal guidelines exist but are advisory), Colorado, Ohio.</p>

  <h2>States with notable caps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Texas:</strong> 5-year cap for most cases; longer for disability</li>
    <li><strong>Florida:</strong> Permanent alimony abolished in 2023; replaced with bridge-the-gap, rehabilitative, or durational support tied to marriage length</li>
    <li><strong>Massachusetts:</strong> Duration percentage tied to marriage length</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Practical implication</h2>
  <p>Where you file matters enormously. A 15-year marriage with a $80,000 income gap could yield wildly different outcomes in California (potentially permanent), Texas (5-year cap, $5,000/month max), or Florida (durational support, no permanent option).</p>
  <p>For your specific state, consult a local family-law attorney or your state's bar association resources.</p>
</BaseLayout>
